    "notes": "Calendar-year NOMINAL annual total returns, 1871-2025, as decimals. Built by tools/ingest/build-market.ts. TIMING: stocks, bonds and inflation are January-to-January. The value for year Y runs from the January Y level to the January Y+1 level, where Shiller's monthly S&P price is the average of daily closes in the month, so \"year Y\" is effectively mid-January Y to mid-January Y+1 (the FIRECalc/cFIREsim convention), not December-to-December. usStocks: S&P Composite total return from Shiller's P and D columns. The twelve monthly total returns from February Y to January Y+1 are compounded; each month's total return is (P_m + D_m/12) / P_(m-1), where D is Shiller's trailing-twelve-month dividend per share, so one twelfth of it is paid and reinvested each month at that month's average price. usBonds: constant-maturity 10-year Treasury total return from Shiller's long interest rate column (\"Rate GS10\"). Each year a 10-year bond is bought at par at the January yield y0, pays one annual coupon y0, and is repriced one year later as a 9-year annual-coupon bond at the next January yield y1: ret = y0 + price(9y, coupon y0, yield y1) - 1. inflation: January-to-January change in Shiller's CPI column (BLS CPI-U, not seasonally adjusted, from 1913; Shiller's spliced Warren-Pearson based index before 1913). cash: 1934 onward: FRED TB3MS (3-Month Treasury Bill Secondary Market Rate, discount basis, monthly; https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/TB3MS) averaged over the twelve months of calendar year Y (January-December, so about half a month ahead of the January-to-January window of the other series) and converted to an annual return by rolling 91-day bills: (1 - d*91/360)^(-365/91) - 1. 1928-1933: Damodaran's published 3-month T-bill return for the year. Before 1928 (APPROXIMATED): calendar-year mean of Shiller's long rate minus a fixed term spread of 1.26 percentage points, floored at zero; the spread is the mean of (long rate - cash) over 1928-2025. Short-term commercial rates before 1928 were often above long government yields, so this proxy probably understates cash returns in that period. The sha256 in this header is of ie_data.xls (the copy linked from https://shillerdata.com/; the older Yale copy at http://www.econ.yale.edu/~shiller/data/ie_data.xls ends in 2023 and is only a fallback). Last month in the workbook: 2026-10; 2025 is the last year with a following-January row. Other raw inputs: TB3MS.csv sha256 33fae406051a658c316580f8e8b5e461157e6663bc03b35648cc7cd3e89ee26b (https://fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/fredgraph.csv?id=TB3MS, retrieved 2026-11-10); histretSP.xlsx sha256 d7245bc8b09cd56cab5505188fe4aaddf6e0385b969310f33a4918019c321196 (https://pages.stern.nyu.edu/~adamodar/pc/datasets/histretSP.xlsx, retrieved 2026-11-10).",
    "flags": {
      "cash": "approximated before 1928: Shiller long rate minus a fixed 1.26-point term spread (floored at 0); 1928-1933 from Damodaran; 1934+ from FRED TB3MS",
